data: {"type":"handoff","handoff":{"text":"{\"v\":1,\"hash\":\"b9c4a57381ea9ddb699ee666755c58f30b5b91b61b304330fddf621f633a2b36\",\"at\":1795354240295,\"retried\":[],\"merged\":{\"byChunk\":[{\"chunkId\":1,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"1.1\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and the fifth crewed Apollo mission.\"},{\"id\":\"1.2\",\"text\":\"Crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Buzz Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight.\"},{\"id\":\"1.3\",\"text\":\"The Saturn V launched from Kennedy Space Center on July 16 at 13:32 UTC.\"},{\"id\":\"1.4\",\"text\":\"The command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part returning to Earth; the service module supplied propulsion, power, oxygen, and water.\"},{\"id\":\"1.5\",\"text\":\"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard Columbia.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module Eagle\",\"Command Module Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\"]},{\"chunkId\":2,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"2.1\",\"text\":\"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later.\"},{\"id\":\"2.2\",\"text\":\"The two spent roughly two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, deploying instruments, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material.\"},{\"id\":\"2.3\",\"text\":\"After more than 21 hours on the surface, they rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and the crew returned safely to Earth on July 24.\"},{\"id\":\"2.4\",\"text\":\"Apollo 11 culminated the Space Race, a Cold War competition between the United States and the Soviet Union, and fulfilled Kennedy's May 1961 goal.\"},{\"id\":\"2.5\",\"text\":\"The program recovered from the fatal Apollo 1 launchpad fire in January 1967 and built on Mercury and Gemini technologies.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Apollo 11\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\"]},{\"chunkId\":3,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"3.1\",\"text\":\"The Soviet Union, despite early spaceflight leads, could not match the Saturn V rocket.\"},{\"id\":\"3.2\",\"text\":\"Soviet uncrewed probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21, while Armstrong and Aldrin were still on the surface.\"},{\"id\":\"3.3\",\"text\":\"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, about one-fifth of the world's population.\"},{\"id\":\"3.4\",\"text\":\"Lunar samples from the mission led to identification of three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments returned data for years.\"},{\"id\":\"3.5\",\"text\":\"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Saturn V\",\"Luna 15\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Eagle\",\"United States\",\"Cold War\"]},{\"chunkId\":4,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"4.1\",\"text\":\"Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1 on October 4, 1957, the first artificial satellite, surprising the world and fueling fears about Soviet capabilities.\"},{\"id\":\"4.2\",\"text\":\"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority.\"},{\"id\":\"4.3\",\"text\":\"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers.\"},{\"id\":\"4.4\",\"text\":\"President Dwight D. Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"4.5\",\"text\":\"Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit Earth on April 12, 1961; Alan Shepard's May 5, 1961 flight was suborbital.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Soviet Union\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"President Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"President John F. Kennedy\",\"Space Race\",\"Sputnik crisis\"]},{\"chunkId\":5,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"5.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.\"},{\"id\":\"5.2\",\"text\":\"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose.\"},{\"id\":\"5.3\",\"text\":\"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s end.\"},{\"id\":\"5.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said no single space project would be more impressive to mankind or more important, and none more difficult or expensive.\"},{\"id\":\"5.5\",\"text\":\"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing larger liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding engine development and unmanned explorations.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Earth\",\"Moon\"]},{\"chunkId\":6,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"6.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy argued that going to the Moon would be a national effort, since all Americans must work to put one man there.\"},{\"id\":\"6.2\",\"text\":\"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University's football stadium in Houston, Texas.\"},{\"id\":\"6.3\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said outer space held no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its conquest deserved the best of all mankind.\"},{\"id\":\"6.4\",\"text\":\"Kennedy framed the Moon goal by comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas, concluding 'We choose to go to the Moon.'\"}],\"entities\":[\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"Moon\",\"outer space\"]},{\"chunkId\":7,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"7.1\",\"text\":\"Kennedy said the nation chooses to go to the Moon this decade because the challenge is hard, and it will organize and measure the best energies and skills.\"},{\"id\":\"7.2\",\"text\":\"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener of MIT dubbed it a \\\"moondoggle\\\".\"},{\"id\":\"7.3\",\"text\":\"The effort to land a man on the Moon was already named Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"7.4\",\"text\":\"In June 1961, Kennedy proposed a joint Moon landing to Premier Nikita Khrushchev, who declined; Kennedy repeated the proposal at the UN in 1963.\"},{\"id\":\"7.5\",\"text\":\"The joint Moon mission idea was abandoned after Kennedy's death; an early key decision was lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Kennedy\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"Soviet Union\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"Moon\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\"]},{\"chunkId\":8,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"8.1\",\"text\":\"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for the Apollo missions.\"},{\"id\":\"8.2\",\"text\":\"The Apollo spacecraft had three major parts: the command module, service module, and lunar module.\"},{\"id\":\"8.3\",\"text\":\"The command module held the three astronauts and was the only part that returned to Earth.\"},{\"id\":\"8.4\",\"text\":\"The lunar module had a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return astronauts to lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"8.5\",\"text\":\"This design allowed the spacecraft to be launched by a single Saturn V rocket then under development; Project Gemini developed required technologies.\"}],\"entities\":[\"NASA\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"Command module (CM)\",\"Service module (SM)\",\"Lunar module (LM)\",\"Saturn V\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\"]},{\"chunkId\":9,\"points\":[{\"id\":\"9.1\",\"text\":\"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee, and halted Project Apollo.\"},{\"id\":\"9.2\",\"text\":\"In October 1968, Apollo 7 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.3\",\"text\":\"In December 1968, Apollo 8 tested the command module in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.4\",\"text\":\"In March 1969, Apollo 9 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit; in May, Apollo 10 performed a 'dress rehearsal' in lunar orbit.\"},{\"id\":\"9.5\",\"text\":\"By July 1969, everything was ready for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon.\"}],\"entities\":[\"Project Apollo\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\",\"Apollo 11\"]}],\"entities\":[\"Apollo 11\",\"NASA\",\"Neil Armstrong\",\"Michael Collins\",\"Buzz Aldrin\",\"Saturn V\",\"Kennedy Space Center\",\"Lunar Module Eagle\",\"Command Module Columbia\",\"Sea of Tranquility\",\"Armstrong\",\"Aldrin\",\"Collins\",\"Richard Nixon\",\"Space Race\",\"United States\",\"Soviet Union\",\"John F. Kennedy\",\"Apollo 1\",\"Mercury\",\"Gemini\",\"Luna 15\",\"Columbia\",\"National Air and Space Museum\",\"Eagle\",\"Cold War\",\"Sputnik 1\",\"President Dwight D. Eisenhower\",\"National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)\",\"Project Mercury\",\"Yuri Gagarin\",\"Alan Shepard\",\"President John F. Kennedy\",\"Sputnik crisis\",\"Kennedy\",\"Eisenhower\",\"United States Congress\",\"Earth\",\"Moon\",\"Rice University\",\"Houston, Texas\",\"outer space\",\"Norbert Wiener\",\"Massachusetts Institute of Technology\",\"Project Apollo\",\"Nikita Khrushchev\",\"United Nations General Assembly\",\"lunar orbit rendezvous\",\"James Webb\",\"Apollo\",\"Command module (CM)\",\"Service module (SM)\",\"Lunar module (LM)\",\"Project Gemini\",\"Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)\",\"Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)\",\"Gus Grissom\",\"Ed White\",\"Roger B. Chaffee\",\"Apollo 7\",\"Apollo 8\",\"Apollo 9\",\"Apollo 10\"],\"findingCount\":9}}","seal":"f1d40a022bff0afdfc61e2ee35ce781a8148c5da6c9e4cff0b9fda6e7a5deb1e"}}

